as a consumer of a product

00:21:16.027 --> 00:21:19.737
compared to a 
non-social media consumer

00:21:19.737 --> 00:21:24.338
is that a social media 
consumer is an advocate.

00:21:25.378 --> 00:21:28.214
They continue the 
building process of the brand

00:21:28.214 --> 00:21:30.516
because they are using the tools

00:21:30.516 --> 00:21:31.751
like Facebook Share,

00:21:31.751 --> 00:21:32.685
like Twitter

00:21:32.685 --> 00:21:33.920
like that,

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:37.390
to continue the 
story about the brand.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:38.224
- [Jay] Quick follow up 
on that, Gary.

00:21:38.224 --> 00:21:39.135
So what you're saying though is

00:21:39.135 --> 00:21:41.845
so that you 
advocate social media

00:21:41.845 --> 00:21:42.812
as more of a customer loyalty

00:21:42.812 --> 00:21:43.747
and retention tactic

00:21:43.747 --> 00:21:46.349
moreso than a 
customer acquisition?

00:21:46.349 --> 00:21:47.396
- [Gary] Yeah. Both.

00:21:47.396 --> 00:21:49.479
It's like players, right?

00:21:49.479 --> 00:21:50.919
Everybody has players.

00:21:50.919 --> 00:21:52.587
Your basketball 
team you have 12 players

00:21:52.587 --> 00:21:54.289
but 12 of them might suck.

00:21:54.289 --> 00:21:55.223
You might have customers

00:21:55.223 --> 00:21:57.158
but they might not be 
doing anything for your brand

00:21:57.158 --> 00:22:01.424
except a one way transaction.

00:22:01.424 --> 00:22:04.835
I feel that social media
consumers are multiple traction

00:22:04.835 --> 00:22:07.060
and can create so much.

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:08.628
Listen, if I love your product

00:22:08.628 --> 00:22:09.629
and I tweet about it

00:22:09.629 --> 00:22:10.530
and do a video blog about it,

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:11.831
things are going to happen.

00:22:11.831 --> 00:22:13.867
And there's a lot of 
people replicating like that

00:22:13.867 --> 00:22:16.475
on many different levels.

00:22:16.475 --> 00:22:18.359
You know what?

00:22:18.359 --> 00:22:19.371
Let me finish with this 
before it rains too hard,

00:22:19.371 --> 00:22:22.066
but we can keep going.

00:22:22.066 --> 00:22:25.497
I don't give a fuck about
one brand that doesn't get it.

00:22:25.497 --> 00:22:27.597
Not for one fucking second.

00:22:27.597 --> 00:22:28.391
You know why?

00:22:28.391 --> 00:22:31.394
Cause they're going to 
fucking lose and be gone.

00:22:31.394 --> 00:22:35.131
I'm not in the business to
protect Pepsi from dying off

00:22:35.131 --> 00:22:36.766
and letting some other 
brand come in and take it.

00:22:36.766 --> 00:22:39.436
because if you 
don't think shit changes

00:22:39.436 --> 00:22:40.537
when platforms change

00:22:40.537 --> 00:22:41.638
just pay attention

00:22:41.638 --> 00:22:43.640
to the five 
biggest selling beers

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:45.075
when radio was in existence

00:22:45.075 --> 00:22:46.843
and what happened 
in the first five years

00:22:46.843 --> 00:22:49.506
of television hitting 
some sort of critical mass.

00:22:49.506 --> 00:22:51.141
Because Schaffer 
and Pabst Blue Ribbon,

00:22:51.141 --> 00:22:52.089
I promise you,

00:22:52.089 --> 00:22:53.691
they would re-do some shit.

00:22:53.691 --> 00:22:55.693
(laughing)

00:22:55.693 --> 00:22:57.962
So the landscape's 
going to change.

00:22:57.962 --> 00:23:00.865
The next Red Bull and Vitamin
Water and blah blah blah

00:23:00.865 --> 00:23:02.466
is gonna come from this space

00:23:02.466 --> 00:23:05.136
and they're gonna spend 
a hell of a lot less money

00:23:05.136 --> 00:23:07.271
than Red Bull and 
Vitamin Water did.

00:23:07.271 --> 00:23:09.440
Somebody's gonna crush it,

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.175
build a fucking 
$100 million brand,

00:23:11.175 --> 00:23:14.245
and do it for 
less than $500,000.

00:23:14.245 --> 00:23:16.113
And that's when 
people are going to be like,

00:23:16.113 --> 00:23:19.477
"Oh, now I get it".
(laughing)

00:23:19.477 --> 00:23:20.311
- [Man 6] Do 
you think the space

00:23:20.311 --> 00:23:21.145
is going to become saturated

00:23:21.145 --> 00:23:23.410
with people who don't 
want to spend any money

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:24.820
and expect great results?

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:25.654
- Yes!

00:23:26.874 --> 00:23:28.342
And then the cream 
will rise to the top

00:23:28.342 --> 00:23:30.177
and they'll get money.

00:23:30.177 --> 00:23:31.412
It's going on right now.

00:23:31.412 --> 00:23:33.147
There's a 
fuckload of shit going on

00:23:33.147 --> 00:23:35.225
but there's only a very 
small tier that makes money.

00:23:35.225 --> 00:23:36.060
Guess what?

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.295
That's how it is out here too!

00:23:38.295 --> 00:23:39.263
- [Man 6] It's 
always been that way.

00:23:39.263 --> 00:23:40.147
- It's always that.

00:23:40.147 --> 00:23:41.682
But everybody thinks 
that if they have a blog

00:23:41.682 --> 00:23:42.516
or if they're on social media

00:23:42.516 --> 00:23:43.718
they're gonna get in 
and they're gonna crush it.

00:23:43.718 --> 00:23:45.052
You've gotta be good

00:23:45.052 --> 00:23:46.296
and you've gotta 
work your ass off.

00:23:46.296 --> 00:23:48.765
It's the same principles 
that have always existed.

00:23:48.765 --> 00:23:50.366
If you suck

00:23:50.366 --> 00:23:52.468
at sports radio talk

00:23:52.468 --> 00:23:54.170
nobody is going to 
listen to your shit

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:55.448
and you're not going 
to be able to monetize.

00:23:55.448 --> 00:23:56.749
So make sure what you do

00:23:56.749 --> 00:23:58.318
is something 
you're passionate about

00:23:58.318 --> 00:23:59.886
and you're good at.

00:24:01.436 --> 00:24:02.270
- [Man 7] Do 
you swear this much

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:03.738
when you talk to 
Pepsi and those big brands?

00:24:03.738 --> 00:24:05.573
- Never. Only when I get people.

00:24:05.573 --> 00:24:06.507
- [Man 7] Do you 
tell them that they suck?

00:24:06.507 --> 00:24:07.975
- Yes. Immediately.

00:24:07.975 --> 00:24:08.810
Immediately.

00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:10.178
- [Man 7] Do they give you
a hard time with that though?

00:24:10.178 --> 00:24:12.847
- I have nothing to 
lose. That's not what I do.

00:24:12.847 --> 00:24:14.649
I do a lot of consulting

00:24:14.649 --> 00:24:15.967
but it's not my 
main source of income.

00:24:15.967 --> 00:24:18.019
So for me to walk in--

00:24:18.019 --> 00:24:20.688
I saw Tim Zagat at the Founders
Meeting the other night,

00:24:20.688 --> 00:24:22.590
and said, "You're 
getting your face beat in".

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.058
That's my opening line to him.

00:24:24.058 --> 00:24:25.026
- [Man 7] He 
smiled at you, right?

00:24:25.026 --> 00:24:26.094
(laughing)

00:24:26.094 --> 00:24:27.995
- And he said to his 
friend, Bernie, from ABC

00:24:27.995 --> 00:24:29.835
who introduced me.
He's like, "I like this kid."

00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:31.036
Because I don't give a fuck

00:24:31.036 --> 00:24:32.104
if he's out of 
business tomorrow.

00:24:32.104 --> 00:24:33.472
I really don't.

00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:34.973
It's not what I can do.

00:24:34.973 --> 00:24:38.310
I don't have the time 
to help him. You know?

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.345
- [Man 8] What kind of 
wine goes good with fish?

00:24:40.345 --> 00:24:43.348
(laughing)

00:24:43.348 --> 00:24:44.320
- White.

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:47.865
Guys, thank you so 
much for coming out.

00:24:47.865 --> 00:24:50.134
(applause)

00:24:52.404 --> 00:24:55.341
Thank you for 
braving the elements.

00:24:56.701 --> 00:24:57.936
I really appreciate it.

00:24:57.936 --> 00:24:59.104
Thank you guys.

00:24:59.104 --> 00:25:00.238
- [Man 9] Thank you, Gary!

00:25:00.238 --> 00:25:02.507
(applause)

